  "slug": "report-technical-4b00",
  "summary": "Recent preprints test whether structure confidence, homology, and length confounds inflate reported performance of biological sequence and structure models.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is whether predictive scores for biological sequences and structures track the property of interest once confounds are controlled. The lanmodulin preprint reports that structural confidence (pTM, ipLDDT) is a poor selection filter for REE-binding designs and points to a function-trained model on measured Tb affinity. The antimicrobial peptide preprint enforces homology separation and length matching and finds that length alone separates classes. ProtCOVER applies family-level conformal calibration to variant effect predictors on unseen protein families, with density-ratio reweighting under descriptor shift. MADM and Calpha extend architecture scope: MADM combines per-residue structural descriptors with PLM embeddings across eight developability assays, while Calpha routes LLM residue states to a SimpleFold structure head for single-chain folding. What is new is explicit scrutiny of what these models exploit and what they are validated against, rather than headline accuracy alone.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
